\section*{Decidable word problem and embedding in finitely presented simple groups}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents a proof in the selected manuscript. Every group with finite generators and a decidable word problem is claimed to embed in a finitely presented simple group.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Finite algebraic data encode the input inside an affine envelope. The construction removes the Steinberg kernel and builds a two-transitive envelope, while a decreasing dependency bound makes the coding finite; simplicity and embedding follow from the resulting action.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The primary theorem proves both directions for every finitely generated G and an injective homomorphism into a finitely presented simple group. lean/docs/250.md documents this exact Boone--Higman equivalence. Within these assumptions, this is a claimed affirmative answer to the stated question.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 250, the documented entries cover Boone--Higman equivalence, simple $F_\infty$ overgroups for decidable word problems, and one universal $F_\infty$ group. The converse characterization of all its recursively presented subgroups is extra manuscript scope. The listed Comparator files are \texttt{BooneHigman.lean}, \texttt{SimpleOvergroups.lean}, \texttt{UniversalFInfinity.lean}. Named declarations include \texttt{OAI.UniversalFInfinity.universal\_group\_of\_type\_FInfinity}. This site has not executed the Lean code. The scope descriptions do not certify the remaining manuscript arguments.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these manuscripts to work by an internal model. Its release cautions that arguments without formalization can contain mistakes. This catalogue has not independently checked the proofs; the labels report the mathematical claims attributed to OpenAI.

